/**
 * HealthConnect Marketplace — gateway.
 *
 * Owns user accounts, JWT issue and verify, routing and rate limiting. No domain entities: every
 * one of those belongs to a service that owns its own schema.
 *
 * Spec: docs/healthconnect-marketplace.md §4. Decisions: docs/decisions.md D1.
 *
 * Import with:
 *   jhipster jdl jdl/gateway.jdl
 *
 * WHY MONGODB, WHEN THE SPEC SAID POSTGRES
 * ----------------------------------------
 * A JHipster gateway is always reactive — it is Spring Cloud Gateway. JHipster 9.2.0 decides the
 * Spring Boot generation with `springBoot4 = !(databaseTypeSql && reactive)`, so a SQL gateway is
 * silently dropped to Boot 3.5.15 while the four SQL microservices get Boot 4.0.7. Verified by
 * probe-generating all three shapes, not by reading documentation.
 *
 * The gateway stores accounts and nothing else, so it has no need of SQL; every place this design
 * actually leans on SQL — the professional_rating view, the ledger aggregates, the
 * same-slice-of-days month-to-date comparison — lives in a domain service, and those stay on
 * PostgreSQL 17. This is also the proven idiom in hc-admin, hc-patient and hc-professional, each
 * of which runs a reactive MongoDB gateway that owns users and issues JWTs.
 *
 * SHARED SIGNING KEY: this gateway issues tokens the four services validate. Keep
 * JWT_BASE64_SECRET out of any per-service .env — a local value silently wins and the symptom is a
 * successful login followed by 401s from the services, which reads as a broken route.
 */

application {
  config {
    baseName healthconnectGateway
    applicationType gateway
    packageName net.jojoaddison
    serverPort 8080
    databaseType mongodb
    prodDatabaseType mongodb
    devDatabaseType mongodb
    authenticationType jwt
    messageBroker kafka
    serviceDiscoveryType consul
    buildTool maven
    clientFramework no
    enableTranslation false
    testFrameworks [cucumber]
  }
}
